            0.026,
            0.837,
            0.043
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 8 — Nature of Matter: Elements, Compounds, and Mixtures"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.923,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "123"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.082,
            0.743,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "From Activity 8.3, we can **infer** that water is composed of two different constituents—hydrogen and oxygen."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.122,
            0.573,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": "**Water Hydrogen + Oxygen￼**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.153,
            0.315,
            0.173
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ever heard of ..."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.181,
            0.743,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": "When electric current is passed through water, it breaks down into hydrogen and oxygen. Is this a chemical change or a physical change? Recall Curiosity, Grade 7, chapter ‘Changes Around Us: Physical and Chemical’."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.743,
            0.146,
            0.841,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.261,
            0.285,
            0.28
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.3.1 Elements"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.283,
            0.745,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "The two substances hydrogen and oxygen formed in Activity 8.3, are pure substances and are termed as **elements**. Each element is made up of identical particles called atoms. These particles are different from the particles of any other element. Elements are substances that cannot be further broken down into simpler substances. They are the building blocks of all matter. Some other examples of elements are gold, silver, sulfur, carbon, etc."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.424,
            0.296,
            0.445
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.457,
            0.648,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": "The atoms of most of the elements cannot exist independently. Two or more such atoms combine and form a stable particle of that element called a molecule. For example, two atoms of hydrogen combine to form one molecule of hydrogen. Similarly, two atoms of oxygen combine to form one molecule of oxygen (Fig. 8.10)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.809,
            0.26,
            0.95,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.443,
            0.809,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.808,
            0.364,
            0.947,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.865,
                0.347,
                0.892,
                0.362
              ],
              "content": "(a)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.865,
                0.44,
                0.891,
                0.456
              ],
              "content": "(b)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.793,
                0.458,
                0.97,
                0.522
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.10: Depiction of molecules of (a) hydrogen; (b) oxygen"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.577,
            0.745,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "Elements can be classified into **metals** and **non-metals**. You have already studied that gold, silver, magnesium, iron, and aluminium are metals, whereas carbon, sulfur, hydrogen, and oxygen are non-metals. Isn't it interesting to know that some elements like silicon and boron have intermediate properties between those of metals and non-metals? They are called **metalloids**, about which you will learn in higher grades."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.718,
            0.29,
            0.739
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.749,
            0.786,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": " The number of elements known at present is 118, and most of them exist in a solid state."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.785,
            0.786,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "Eleven elements exist in a gaseous state at room temperature, all of which are non-metals like oxygen, helium, nitrogen, etc."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.822,
            0.785,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": " Only two elements are liquid at room temperature—mercury, which is a metal and bromine, which is a non-metal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.858,
            0.786,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": " Although gallium and caesium are solid elements, they become liquid at a temperature around 30 °C (303 K) and turn into liquid."
        },
        {
